{
    "$comment": "The record tree. A key's layer is the number of leading zero BITS of sha256(key), divided by two — so the tree's shape follows from the keys and two servers holding the same records build the same tree without coordinating. A node is {\"e\": [entries], \"l\": link-or-null}; an entry is {\"p\": bytes shared with the previous key, \"k\": the remaining key BYTES (not text), \"v\": the record link, \"t\": link-or-null to keys between this one and the next}. A layer with no keys of its own is not skipped: a node is written holding nothing but a downward link. `root` is the tree over `records`; `nodeCount` is how many nodes that takes. This implementation was checked against a live repository of 1672 records, rebuilding all 463 of its nodes under the names its own network had given them.",
